## 4. Discussion
Cell fate decisions are regulated, in part, by connexin-mediated gap junctional intercellular communication [20,29,39]. In this study, we examined the gene expression profiles for 11 of the 21 reported human $C\mathbf{x}$ isoforms [39–42] in control human iPSCs and after differentiation toward the three embryonic germ layers. Our transcript analysis revealed several significant changes for select $C\mathrm{x}s$ in ectoderm, mesoderm, or endoderm specification. Previous work in human iPSCs revealed gene expression of $C{\times}25$ , $C x26$ , $\mathsf{C x30}$ , $C{\bf x}30.2$ , $C{\bf x}30.3$ , $C{\bf x}31$ , $C{\bf x}31.1$ , $C{\bf x}31.9$ , $C{\bf x}32$ [21,43], $C x36$ , $C{\bf x}37$ , $\mathtt{C x40}.$ , $C{\bf x}43$ , $\mathrm{Cx}45$ , Cx46, $C x47$ , $C{\bf x}59_{\cdot}$ , and $C{\bf x}62$ (summarized in Figure 9) [7,38]. On the other hand, only $C{\bf x}43$ , and $C{\bf x}45$ have been identified at the protein level in human ESCs [21,44,45]. Our qPCR screen revealed expression of $C x26,$ , $C{\bf x}30.3$ , $C{\bf x}31$ , $C{\bf x}31.1$ , $C{\bf x}32$ , $C x36$ , $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Cx37}}$ , $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Cx40}}$ , $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Cx}}43$ , $C{\bf x}45_{.}$ , and $C{\bf x}62$ in control human iPSCs (Figure 1). Given the $C\mathbf{x}$ expression profile revealed by ourselves andCoxth62erisn, ictornetrmoalihnsu pmoasnsibPlSeCtsha(tFisgeuvrera1l) .dGififveerenntthceoCnxn exipnriesosfiornmpsrowfiolerkretvoegaeltehde rbtyo ocuorosredlivneasteancedllo–tcheellrsc,oimt rmeumnaiicnastiopnosisnibhluemthant isPeSvCers.
hLeirkteopcroeovirodiunsartepcoerllts–,cewllecfionmdmthuanti $\mathtt{C x43}$ niisnexhpurmesasneidPiSnCus.ndifferentiated iPSCs and can bLeikreapdrielvyioduetsercetepdoratts,thweepfriontdeitnhlaetvCelxi4n3 icselelsxpfroesmseadllitnhruenedgifefremrelnitnieataegdesi [P2S2C,2s7a].nIdn ciaPnSCbes raenad itlhyedtehtrecet egderatmtlhaeyeprso, $\mathtt{C x43}$ vperloitenicnelloscfarliozmedaltlotthreeceglelrsmurlfiancea,gfeosr [m2i2n,g27l]a.rIgne igPaSpCjsuanctdi othneptlharqeueegse(rFimgluaryee2r)s. DCexs4p3itpercotenitninluoecadliezxepdretsostihoen  ctehlrlosugrfhaocuet, fdoifrfemriengt ilatrigoen, we find that $\mathtt{C x43}$ is dispensable for directed germ layer formation. This contrasts with previous studies which report that $\mathtt{C x43}$ is upregulated during endoderm differentiation and downregulated in ectoderm [22,27]. Indeed, the role of $\mathtt{C x43}$ in germ lineage specification includes several contrasting reports. Peng et al. 2019 demonstrated that shRNA knockdown of $\mathtt{C x43}$ has no impact on endoderm formation, as the knockdown cells were able to express definitive endoderm markers SOX17, FOXA2, and CXCR4 [22]. On the other hand, Yang et al. 2019 demonstrate that siRNA-mediated knockdown impedes definitive endoderm formation from human ESCs. Using CRISPR-Cas9 gene ablation, we find that $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Cx}}43$ knockout iPSCs readily differentiate into all three germ lineages, with no significant difference from wildtype control cells. These discrepancies between our study and the previously published reports might result from our use of CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing rather than RNA interference knockdown methods. However, if that is the case, one would postulate that our knockout cells would exhibit a greater effect compared to previous knockdown studies. Another possibility is that other connexin isoforms could be upregulated to compensate for the loss of $C{\bf x}43$ . As reviewed in Figure 9, few studies have investigated the presence of $\operatorname{Cx}$ proteins in iPSCs, and thereby the description of established GJIC in iPSCs remains limited. Gap junction coupling is commonly evaluated using small dyes and molecules, such as Lucifer yellow and neurobiotin [28,46]. Passage of these dyes and molecules is largely governed by channel composition, as it determines pore size, channel shape, and voltage gating. As such, various tracer dyes have been reported to be more permissible to $\mathtt{C x43}$ channels than $\mathtt{C x40}$ or $C{\bf x}45$ [47,48]. To broaden our investigation, we chose to use tracer dyes, Lucifer yellow and neurobiotin, as these molecules differ in their molecular weight and charge. Our screen of 10 other connexin isoforms, combined with our dye transfer experiments indicate that there is no GJIC compensation happening in our system. Future studies could make use of electrophysiological methods as a more definitive measure of gap junction coupling in $C{\bf x}43$ knockout iPSCs. Furthermore, future studies will determine whether the expression of any of the other Cxs not examined here are altered in our $\mathtt{C x43}$ CRISPR-ablated iPSCs. Finally, there is a growing body of work surrounding internally transcribed $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Cx}}43$ isoforms [49]. Our CRISPR gRNAs target the first two thirds of the GJA1 transcript, leaving the possibility of an internally transcribed $C{\bf x}43$ isoform which may continue to perform non-junctional functions within the cell.
While $\mathtt{C x43}$ has been well studied, there remains much to learn about the other 20 human $C\mathbf{x}$ isoforms, especially given the link of certain $C\mathrm{xs}$ to developmental processes and disease. For example, $\mathtt{C x40}$ mutations are associated with atrial fibrillation [50] and loss of functional $C{\bf x}45$ channels results in abnormal cardiomyocyte contraction and improper cardiac development [18]. $C x26$ mutations are responsible for nearly half of all hereditary deafness cases [51], highlighting the importance of gap junctions for tissue function. To date, limited roles for connexins in stem cell fate decisions have been described. Overexpression of $C{\bf x}43$ in human ESCs positively mediates cardiac differentiation, while mesenchymal stem cells deficient in $\mathtt{C x43}$ exhibit decreased osteoblast differentiation potential [52,53]. Conversely, expression of $\mathtt{C x}32$ markedly decreases in the early stages of adipose-derived stem cell differentiation [54]. Interestingly, overexpression of $\mathtt{C x32}$ and knockdown of $\mathtt{C x43}$ promotes hepatocyte differentiation, highlighting the dynamic relationship between GJIC and cell fate specification [26]. Therefore, it is apparent that Cxs are important for the maintenance and downstream specification of somatic stem cell populations. Given that many $C\mathrm{xs}$ isoforms are associated with human disease, understanding how they work at the cellular level will provide necessary insights into disease pathology but also reveal how they contribute to cellular differentiation and tissue patterning during development.
Implantation of the human embryo is followed by dramatic spatial rearrangements, such as those viewed during gastrulation. Large spatial rearrangement of post-implantation mouse embryos has been shown to be driven by positional cues passed between the embryonic epiblast and the extraembryonic trophectoderm [55,56]. Introducing newer, more sophisticated model systems into future studies would give a more accurate investigation of signaling processes occurring in vivo. The addition of blastoid and embryoid models would complement this study, through the inclusion of influential trophectoderm specific $C\mathbf{x}.$ -mediated signals [57,58]. Furthermore, models, such as gastruloids, would add spatioaxial signals possibly lacking in our current embryoid body models [59].
Several reports dispute the role of $C\mathrm{xs}$ in the establishment, survival, and maintenance of human PSCs [7,20,22,29,38,60–62]. For example, dye transfer assays demonstrate the reestablishment of GJIC during iPSC reprogramming [62], and siRNA knockdown of either $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Cx}}43$ or $C{\bf x}45$ has been shown to negatively impact iPSC reprogramming efficiency [38,60]. Furthermore, ectopic expression of $\mathtt{C x43}$ and $C{\bf x}45$ enhances iPSC reprogramming efficiency [29,38,60,63,64]. However, while broad-spectrum GJIC pharmacological inhibition via carbenoxolone kills human iPSCs, it appears that this occurs independently from $C{\bf x}43$ as our $G J A1-/-$ iPSCs exhibit comparable morphology and survival in culture relative to control iPSCs (Figure 3) [20]. Comprehensive evaluation of how different $C\mathbf{x}$ isoforms contribute to human PSC survival and potency will help resolve these discrepancies. Additional studies using precise genetic ablation of various connexin isoforms, rather than broad pharmacological blockade, will clarify the role played by $C\mathrm{xs}$ in regulating the self-renewal and differentiation capacity of human pluripotent stem cells.
## 5. Conclusions
In conclusion, we present the differential transcript expression for 11 of the $21\mathrm{Cx}$ isoforms in iPSCs and the three germ layers. Despite significant upregulation of several $C x$ transcripts in various germ lineages, along with significant upregulation of $\mathtt{C x43}$ protein in mesoderm, we conclude that $\mathtt{C x43}$ -mediated GJIC is dispensable during lineage specification of human iPSCs.
